videos.script:
A package says it comes from you. A signature is how anybody can check that. {{signature}}

It proves two things: who made the file, and that nobody has changed it since. It proves nothing about whether the app is any good. {{chain}}

On Android the key is the app's identity for life. Lose it, and no update can replace the app on anybody's phone. So you make one, and you keep it twice. {{keystore}}

Apple's chain has four parts, and it runs only on a Mac. You will read it rather than run it. {{apple}}

On the desktop, Windows sells you a certificate, and Linux leaves it to you: a list of checksums, and your signature on the list. {{gpg}} Change one character, and the check says so.

Then the stores, their forms, and the one rule a wrapped web app has to earn its way past. {{review}}
